    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recent generative models have significantly advanced synthetic pathology imaging, addressing challenges such as limited annotations, class imbalance, and domain shifts.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The translation of generative models into clinical practice is constrained by systemic barriers</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Content generation models in computational pathology have undergone a rapid evolution over the past decade, marked by successive paradigm shifts driven by architectural breakthroughs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recent generative models have significantly advanced synthetic pathology imaging</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">These comparisons highlight the steep escalation in training costs as models progress from lightweight GANs to diffusion and foundation-scale architectures.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">training costs as models progress from lightweight GANs to diffusion and foundation-scale architectures.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Content generation models in computational pathology have undergone a rapid evolution over the past decade</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Generative models are trained to approximate data distributions rather than to establish verifiable inputoutput mappings</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">enabling the generator to generate images that are similar to the real images in the target domain</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Generative models are trained to approximate data distributions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GANs (Goodfellow et al., 2014) are able to learn high delity and diverse data representations from a target distribution.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">generative cell simulation offers a targeted means of alleviating annotation bottlenecks in biomedical imaging by producing synthetic datasets</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The model is capable of generating text-based diagnosis reports for WSIs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">By generating histology images conditioned on molecular profiles, these models provide visual hypotheses</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">models progress from lightweight GANs to diffusion and foundation-scale architectures.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Utilizing generative models to create in-silico data</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">By generating histology images conditioned on molecular profiles, these models provide visual hypotheses for how molecular states shape tissue architecture</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Realistic data enrichment from masks for robust segmentation.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Generative artificial intelligence is increasingly reshaping computational pathology</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">our review is centered on content generation models as a distinct methodological paradigm.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PathologyGAN does reproduce these distributions not only when it is judged by convolutional features (32 . 05 / 16 . 65) but also when we use an external tool to directly quantify cellular information</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The flexibility of diffusion models has also facilitated unified, multi-task architectures capable of any-to-any stain conversion</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DDPMs are a collection of generative models that produce high-quality images through iterative denoising.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PathologyGAN The Frechet Inception Distance (FID) (Heusel et al., 2017) is a common metric used to measure GANs performance</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">demonstrates the capability of our pre-trained model to generate histology slices</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Generative artificial intelligence is increasingly reshaping computational pathology, impacting diagnostic imaging, knowledge extraction, and clinical decision-making.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models adopt masked denoising [16], further enhanced by Transformerbased context modeling [51] and latent-space optimization [107]</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
